 so far", "Ratings consensus and prediction markets both point to a Likely Democratic hold", "Even-PVI district and a lone September poll showing only a three-point lead keep the race competitive", "National GOP investment, including Speaker Johnson campaigning for Cunningham and heavy attack ads, targets the seat"], "rationale": "Vasquez benefits from a strong fundraising edge, incumbent perquisites, and a consensus of raters and prediction markets placing the race in Likely Democratic territory, while a favorable midterm environment adds to his side of the ledger. The district's even partisan lean and thin polling showing only a narrow lead prevent a stronger rating.", "watch": "A late wave of Republican outside spending or a strong Cunningham debate performance could tighten what is still a single-digit race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Vasquez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "New Mexico's second district sits at even partisan lean, and incumbent Gabe Vasquez has kept it fiercely competitive since flipping it. He enters the fall with a large financial edge, roughly $2.4 million on hand to Greg Cunningham's $794,000, and has spent recent weeks highlighting water infrastructure wins and constituent service while national figures from both parties campaign in the district.\n\nRaters mostly call the race Likely Democratic, with two Lean Democratic ratings and one Safe Democratic rating alongside; prediction markets put Democratic win odds near ninety percent. The only recent poll, from early September, showed Vasquez ahead by three points, and Republican-leaning spring polling showed a dead heat. Attack ads are flooding the airwaves and House Speaker Mike Johnson has stumped for Cunningham, so the race is live, but the weight of money, ratings, and markets points to a Vasquez hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8892, "margin": 7.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8688, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.870